<BaseLayout
  title="Building This Library · America's School Trust Library"
  description="The institutional formation of America's School Trust Library: completed state incorporation and the next steps toward an independent public institution."
  sourceId="about-becoming"
  feedbackKind="institutional_formation"
>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-a:text-blue-700 prose-a:underline">
    <p class="kicker">About</p>
    <h1>Building This Library</h1>

    <p>
      America's School Trust Library now has a legal home of its own. Oregon
      accepted its Articles of Incorporation on July 21, 2026, creating an
      Oregon nonprofit public benefit corporation (Registry No. 259005891).
      The federal tax-exemption process is under way; no determination has
      been issued.
    </p>

    <h2>Why an independent institution</h2>

    <p>
      The Library grew out of Oregon Advocates for School Trust Lands, but a
      national library must be answerable to its readers and beneficiaries,
      not to a single lawsuit or founder. Independence protects the record,
      invites collaboration across institutional lines, and gives the Library
      a structure designed to outlive any one person, case, or political
      season.
    </p>

    <h2>What has been completed</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Oregon accepted the Articles of Incorporation on July 21, 2026.</li>
      <li>The corporation is a public benefit corporation with no members.</li>
      <li>The Articles contain the provisions required for a charitable, educational organization.</li>
      <li>A founding-board package—bylaws, a constitution and Reader's Bill of Rights, and a conflict-of-interest policy—has been prepared for board action.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What comes next</h2>

    <ol>
      <li>The incorporator appoints the founding directors.</li>
      <li>The directors execute their organizational consent, elect officers, and adopt the governing instruments.</li>
      <li>The corporation obtains an Employer Identification Number and opens its institutional records.</li>
      <li>The board approves and files the appropriate federal tax-exemption application.</li>
      <li>The board develops the broader, independent governing body the Library needs for the long term.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      Until those board actions are signed, this site remains a preview
      publication. The filing creates the corporation; it does not by itself
      seat the proposed directors or adopt the proposed governing documents.
    </p>

    <h2>An open invitation</h2>

    <p>
      Readers, librarians, archivists, educators, trust professionals, and
      peer institutions are still invited to help shape the durable
      institution. Use the <strong>Submit Feedback</strong> control on this
      page, or begin with the contact paths on the <a href="/about/">About</a>
      page. This page is not a fundraising solicitation, and the Library does
      not yet represent that contributions are federally tax deductible.
    </p>

    <p class="signature">— <em>Dave Sullivan, President, OASTL</em></p>

    <p class="page-stamp">
      <em>Page last updated: 2026-07-30.</em>
    </p>
  </article>

